      "sha256": "2317421b2b56766a86b6866a08decd2eedee1b673dca4d91cd71bcad5b9c58e2",
      "identification": "Tunna JSP webshell 1.1a",
      "verdict": "MALICIOUS",
      "evidence": [
        "The original SHA-256 is byte-identical to upstream SECFORCE/Tunna conn.jsp. Header identifies v1.1a and the author; the publisher documents it as a firewall-bypassing webshell/pivoting tool.",
        "The proxy request branch reads multipart HTTP bytes into a buffer, selects a filename from the upload, prefixes java.io.tmpdir and writes FileOutputStream. It stores that path in the HTTP session.",
        "The next session request retrieves the same path, constructs chmod +x on non-Windows hosts, obtains Runtime.getRuntime(), and calls r.exec(new String[]{s,Integer.toString(port)}). A temporary ServerSocket selects a port, then closes; it is not a bind shell.",
        "Alternatively, request ip/port values select a TCP target. A nonblocking SocketChannel is held in the HTTP session. Subsequent requests transfer request bytes through ByteBuffer.wrap to that socket and return socket bytes through the servlet response.",
        "Close deletes the uploaded file, destroys the launched SOCKS process and invalidates the session. No authentication or operator-approval gate exists in this page. Java == comparisons against empty strings and null-handling bugs may affect runtime branches but do not erase the implemented backdoor capability.",
        "Authorized penetration testing is a valid explanation. No such deployment context was supplied. An unauthenticated upload/execute and network-pivot server page favors MALICIOUS when discovered on a machine; upstream origin alone does not authorize its installation."
      ],
